diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index df93a97..42e8de0 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -2,6 +2,19 @@
 
 A740 kernel GPU driver.
 
+The shared ring, CP initializer, IRQ decoder and timer also accept the explicit
+`NativeAdrenoChipA8xx` register profile, adapted from myron's `4056854`.
+The A840 board backend supplies its own power, firmware and context resources.
+A8xx currently uses one ring and CPU page-table switching; A740 preemption
+records and CP table-switch packets are rejected for that profile. Host checks
+are separate from A840 hardware qualification.
+
+Ring profile selection validates every register field and retains its own
+canonical table. Apple kernel links may retain two equal inline constants at
+different addresses; pointer identity is not a hardware identity check. The
+ring regression includes equivalent copies, caller lifetime and every changed
+field, while unknown profiles and selection after attachment remain refused.
+
 The implementation covers A740 on SM8550. GPU commands and scanout copy have been verified on sheng. Platform inputs provide firmware, power policy, and memory resources.
 
 Two priority rings preserve queue FIFO and per-ring page tables. Context switching uses the executed-table snapshot, GMEM save/restore and a preemption postamble. The host scheduling check covers packet sequencing, priority changes and fence-history rollover; board policy selects the mode.
@@ -10,6 +23,74 @@ Build and interface details: [documentation](https://unixnu.com/drivers/adreno74
 
 ## Frame diagnostics
 
+With GPU tracing enabled, plugin phase markers and completed PID-scoped text
+lines also reach the existing kernel console/USB sink. The optional0x7100
+terminator preserves complete lines across interleaved processes; older senders
+flush on their next line. Truncation/capacity loss is explicit. A separate CPU
+observer samples the existing software counters and changed telemetry rows;
+it never waits on the GPU queue lock, accesses MMIO or performs recovery.
+These diagnostic additions are not a GPU performance or hardware qualification.
+
+Repeated fence and frame-wait timeout lines retain every occurrence in atomic
+counters, print their first four and powers of two, and report changing totals
+from the observer. Other RPC errors remain immediate. This leaves all RPC
+results and waits unchanged and prevents ordinary polling from flooding the
+console drain. The observer also copies at most eight previously unseen
+event-65 metadata texts per pass and prints them after releasing the metadata
+latch; the 120-byte trace ABI and complete trace ring remain unchanged.
+
+The compatible upstream GMEM trace selection also retains events36,37 and44
+(pass, tile-memory transfer and attachment) when the sender enables tracing.
+The120-byte record ABI is unchanged; this does not enable A740 preemption on A840.
+
 `sheng-gpu-trace=1` enables the kernel GPU ring: submission, retirement, display arrival, display fence and latch, build identity, and the render server's render, encode and completion thread identities, 65536 records. Repeated pending display queries are coalesced. See [GPU frame diagnostics](https://unixnu.com/porting/gpu-diagnostics) for fields and decoding.
 
 `NativeGpuPerfCounters.h` defines the opt-in 19-counter A7XX BR plan used by sheng diagnostics. Capture records selector readback, unstable 64-bit reads, reset masks and preemption epochs. The host check verifies the register plan against pinned Mesa XML.
+
+
+The CPU console observer also exports a bounded, coherent copy of the existing
+stall sample: CP and SMMU state, nearby IB words, mapping-handle lifetime clues,
+the owning job/process, selected page table and recovery counters. It performs
+no register access and never waits for the GPU lock. Its sequence check rejects
+an in-progress copy. The sample records any preceding unstick stage explicitly;
+it is not represented as a pre-intervention hardware snapshot. Timeout and
+recovery counts remain poll/check counts, not counts of distinct failed jobs.
+
+A840 CPU page-table changes and VM teardown now wait for the existing ring
+idle check (RPTR drained and RBBM quiet) after the job timestamps complete.
+Timeouts leave the old table installed; no fence is fabricated or GPU reset
+added. This closes a source-level handoff gap; the Control Center hang still
+requires hardware retesting. A bounded stall-only board reader makes system
+BO command words available without adding mappings to normal submissions.
+Active IB addresses are matched against the retained job before using a
+prefetched ring descriptor as a fallback.
+
+The optional performance snapshot exposes cumulative job, submission, cache
+and lock times plus XNU CPU-load counters sampled by the existing observer.
+The existing CP_ALWAYS_ON stamps measure the command-stream interval around
+user IBs; asynchronous tail/cache draining is separate, so this is not labeled
+shader-only or end-to-end frame time. Stamps whose circular slot could have
+been reused carry bit32 and are omitted from cumulative GPU timing. USB
+integration stays in the board; six-record trace pages reuse the existing
+nonblocking store and report missed/overwritten slots.
+
+## Complete submissions
+
+ABI2 method19 stages up to1024 IB descriptors in the existing3104-byte
+transport envelope, then commits them as one GPU job. BEGIN/APPEND never
+publish ring commands. COMMIT validates every live handle and range, emits
+one complete framing sequence and consumes its token; only an explicit Busy
+refusal permits retry. Abort and connection teardown discard unsubmitted
+staging, while the existing fence rules keep submitted BOs alive. Method5's
+128-entry wire layout is unchanged. The new capability flag requires a
+matching updated client; callers must not fall back to arbitrary IB splits.
+
+This addresses a submission-contract gap found in nezha calculator stalls:
+140 and150 IBs were split at128, the first job completed and the second
+stalled. Other split submissions completed, so the count alone is not a
+proven hardware cause. Host tests execute the production dispatch, VM,
+execbuf and ring over software peers, including the two observed sizes,
+the1024-entry limit, stale/repeated tokens, partial uploads, Busy retry and
+resource lifetime. Lists that cannot fit an empty ring return a permanent
+argument error rather than a retryable full-ring result. Hardware retesting
+is still required; no barrier, fence or recovery rule was removed.
